      </p>

      <h2>16. Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about these Terms: <a href="mailto:team@nosana.io">team@nosana.io</a>.
      </p>
      {/*
        NOTES FOR REVIEW: internal only, not rendered. Confirm each item, then remove.

        - Counterparty entity: this Terms of Service is between the user and NOSANA B.V. (the operating
          company), NOT the Nosana Foundation. The Privacy Policy remains under the Foundation. Following the
          Akash pattern, the entity is named once (in the opening line) but deliberately NOT identified: no
          registered address or KvK number is given. TRADE-OFF: Akash gets away with this partly as a US
          entity. A Dutch BV serving EU consumers is a "trader," and EU consumer / e-commerce rules generally
          require the trader's identity and geographic address to be made available to consumers. You can keep
          that out of the ToS body (as here), but you likely still need it reachable somewhere, e.g., a legal-
          notice / imprint page or the contact section of the site. Confirm the BV is correctly and legally
          named, and that identity/address is available elsewhere, with counsel.

        - Split entity (KEEP THIS TRUE): ToS = BV, Privacy Policy = Foundation. This split only holds up if the
          FOUNDATION genuinely remains the data controller (i.e., it decides how personal data is processed). If
          in practice the BV makes the data-processing decisions (it operates the site, logs, and payments), the
          BV is the de facto controller and the Privacy Policy's controller should move to the BV. Confirm with
          counsel which entity actually controls the data, and align the Privacy Policy accordingly.

        - EU footprint (deliberate): a Netherlands BV is EU-established, so this operating relationship sits under
          full GDPR by establishment and Dutch/EU consumer-protection law. That is the trade-off of choosing a
          Dutch BV over an offshore entity, and it was chosen knowingly. Governing law, courts, liability floor
          and currency below were all set to match a Dutch entity.

        - Governing law / disputes: set to Netherlands law + competent courts of Amsterdam, which is the natural
          and coherent choice for a Dutch BV. NOTE on arbitration: unlike the offshore-foundation route, a broad
          binding-arbitration + class-action-waiver clause (as Akash uses) is generally NOT enforceable against
          EU consumers and can be struck down under EU consumer law. For a Dutch BV facing consumers, the court
          route is usually the safer and often mandatory choice. Confirm with counsel before adding arbitration.

        - Liability cap + currency: cap formula (greater of six months' fees or €100) is standard and matches
          Akash's own terms almost verbatim. Currency set to euros to match the Dutch entity. Standard carve-outs
          added so the cap does not shield the user's indemnification, prohibited-use, or IP-infringement
          obligations. The consumer carve-out references Netherlands law / mandatory consumer rights. Confirm the
          figure with counsel, bearing in mind mandatory EU consumer protections.

        - Services in scope: now anchored (Akash-style) to the Website (nosana.com) and the Platform
          (deploy.nosana.com), with a catch-all for other services/software/features. This means the SDK
          (@nosana/kit), the CLI (@nosana/cli), the host software, and apps like Explore/Host/Stake are no longer
          named individually; they fall under the catch-all. Confirm that is intended; name them explicitly if
          you want them clearly in scope.

        - Contact address: team@nosana.io is used. Confirm this inbox is monitored for legal notices, or route
          legal notices to a dedicated address.

        - Minimum age: set at a straightforward 18+ with no supervised-minor carve-out, which is cleaner for a
